# Use clear language

Write so anyone can read a notification, card or dialog once and know what to do.

<h2 id="write-for-the-reader-you-have">Write for everyone in the home</h2>

Everyone in the home reads Home Assistant text, including guests and children, often when tired, rushed or worried. Some read English as a second language.

<%= partial 'partials/four_nations_reading' %>

Many people find numbers hard too. Read [Write numbers, measurements, dates and time](numbers-measurements-dates-time.html).

<h2 id="write-clearly-for-experienced-users">Write clearly for experienced users too</h2>

Experienced Home Assistant users still prefer plain words: they read them faster. Others in the home read their notifications too.

<h2 id="wg-010">Use everyday words</h2>

Use the short word: “start”, not “initiate”; “about”, not “approximately”.

Turn “-ion” and “-ment” words back into verbs. Replace vague words such as “optimise” with what happens. In a repair:

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Completion of the update installation requires a restart. | Restart Home Assistant to finish the update. |

<h2 id="home-assistant-words-in-use">Explain Home Assistant terms the first time</h2>

Keep a Home Assistant term when people need it, and explain it in plain words the first time. Check terms in [A to Z Technical](../style-guides/technical-a-to-z.html).

<%= example('term-device-entity') %>

<%= example('term-power-energy') %>

<h2 id="use-the-words-people-use">Use the words people in the home use</h2>

Name things the way the household does: “heating”, not “climate control”; “bin day”, not “waste collection schedule”.

<h3 id="wg-020">Use one word for one action</h3>

Once a button says “Turn off”, do not call the same action “Switch off” or “Deactivate” elsewhere. If the living room lamp card has a “Turn off” button, the notification that the lamp is still on offers “Turn off” too.

<h3 id="wg-021">Keep native labels and translations</h3>

Use the label Home Assistant shows, in its language: “Go to **Settings** > **Devices & services**”, not “open the integrations page”.

<h3 id="wg-022">Keep names exactly as people typed them</h3>

A device named “Mam’s heater” stays “Mam’s heater” everywhere. Do not correct or translate it.

<h3 id="wg-025">Keep literal values literal</h3>

Put entity IDs, keys and paths in code formatting and copy them exactly: `light.reading_lamp`, not light.reading-lamp.

<h2 id="wg-014">Be careful with contractions</h2>

“You’ll” and “it’s” are fine. Write “cannot” and “do not” in full, especially in warnings and conditions. Readers can miss “n’t” and do the opposite. In a dialog warning:

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Don’t unplug the hub while it’s updating. | Do not unplug the hub until the update finishes. |

<h2 id="make-requirements-clear">Make requirements clear</h2>

- Use “must” only for a firm safety or legal requirement: “You must turn off the power at the consumer unit before fitting a smart switch.”
- Use “need” when the task cannot go on without something: “You need a Zigbee coordinator to add Zigbee devices.”
- Use “can” for an option: “You can add the sensor to an area later.” Avoid “you may be able to”.

<h2 id="use-the-active-voice">Use the active voice</h2>

Say who acts: Home Assistant, the device or a person. It is shorter. In a notification:

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Your heating schedule has been paused by Home Assistant until 06:00. | Home Assistant paused your heating schedule until 06:00. |

Use the passive when the result matters more than who acted: “The bin day reminder was turned off.”

<h2 id="wg-013">Keep sentences and paragraphs short</h2>

One idea per sentence, with any condition before the action. Split sentences over 25 words. Keep paragraphs to 5 sentences. In a notification:

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The heating in the living room was turned off by Home Assistant at 18:05 because the window sensor reported the window open for more than 10 minutes. | The living room window was open for more than 10 minutes. Home Assistant turned off the heating at 18:05. |

<h2 id="wg-060">Do not assume who is reading</h2>

Do not assume a relationship, ownership, ability or who is at home. Write “The garage door has been open for 20 minutes”, not “Ask Dad to close the garage”. To write about a person’s age, disability or other personal details, read [Inclusive content](../inclusive-content/index.html).

<h3 id="wg-062">Use respectful, specific language</h3>

Describe what happens, not what a person is like. Leave out “simply”, “obviously” and “just”. Write “Turn the router off, wait 30 seconds, then turn it on”, not “Simply restart the router”.

<h3 id="wg-063">Do not assume one way to see, hear or act</h3>

Do not rely on colour, sound, position or a gesture alone. Write “Select **Turn on**”, not “Tap the green button on the right”.

<h2 id="wg-065">Keep each language separate and checked</h2>

Give each language its own wording, written or checked by a fluent speaker: the button says “Save” in English and “Cadw” in Welsh, never “Save / Cadw”. Do not publish unchecked machine translation.
